he self is the care of the  activity  and not the care of the soul as  substance so we're trying to figure out  well how  how does one figure out what the self is  in movement inactivity and choosing in  the middle of the screwed up  life that i'm already in and that i've  made a number of choices about right so  there's a dialectical  determination that's taking place  there's also a contemplation  that needs to happen as well he says  um what does this care consist  you have to know what the with the soul  consists of the soul cannot know itself  except by looking at itself in a similar  element  a mirror so here's a kind of a leap thus  it must contemplate the divine element  in this divine contemplation the soul  will be able to discover rules to serve  as a basis  for just behavior and political action  and so there's this contemplative aspect  to it as well which goes you know back  to the cardinal  virtue of wisdom and  a bit later um in the the  the piece foucault is going to say  in plato the themes of contemplation of  self  and care of self are related  dialectically  through dialogue and so this is a very  important point  in part because foucault frames it as if  this is something that's that's more  platonic than belonging to these other  perspectives where for example you might  just shut up and listen for  a while before engaging and asking  questions  you know your relationship to the the  teacher the the master  in uh socratic and platonic  dialectic there's a continual back and  forth uh you know reassessment a let's  figure  this out together and that can sometimes  be missing  in in later uh ways of  understanding the the care of the self  so that is uh  an important aspect he tells us that  there's four  key themes or problems  that are arising out of plato's  alcibiades and these are  less sort of like here's the answer more  like here's the questions you need to  figure out through this dialectical work  so one is the relationship of the care